load, M = 0.42 m), as compared with singletask driving, (no load, M = 0.32 m), t(20) = 6.1, p <.001. There was also a significant effect of road, F(2, 40) = 11.1, p < .001, with increased error in lane keeping on curved sections of roadway (M = 0.45 m) as compared with straight rural (M = 0.39 m), t(20) = 3.5, p = .002, and urban stretches (M = 0.33 m), t(20) = 4.5, p < .001. There was, however, no overall effect of display, F(3, 60) = 0.55, p = .65, nor were the two- and three-way interactions significant (ps = .36–.66). The lack of any interactions suggested that given the obligatory costs for dual-task performance, there were no additional performance costs for the different displays in the more challenging road and IVT conditions. We further explored vehicle control by examining the variability in lane keeping during IVT task intervals. A repeated-measures ANOVA revealed a significant effect of display, F(3, 60) = 10.6, p < .001, road, F(2, 40) = 52.2, p < .001, and task load, F(3, 60) = 42.8, p < .001. As shown in Figure 2, there was increased variability in tracking performance in the HDD (M = 0.14 m) and auditory conditions (M = 0.15 m) relative to the adjacent condition (M = 0.13 m), t(20) = 3.4, p = .003, and t(20) = 3.8, p = .001, respectively. There were, however, no differences between the adjacent and overlay (M = 0.12 m) conditions, t(20) = 1.3, p = .22. As shown by the significant Display × Road interaction, F(6, 120) = 3.5, p = .003, there was particularly
